  "strategy": "1. **Approach and Rationale**  \nThis approach uses a simulated annealing algorithm to iteratively minimize the overlap integral $C_5$. The key steps are:  \n   - Start from the provided parent solution (n=45) and use local search (swaps) to explore the space.  \n   - Double the grid size whenever improvements plateau, leveraging function projection to maintain feasibility.  \n   - **Bottleneck**: The objective function (overlap integral) is expensive to compute, requiring careful balance between computation time and search quality. This approach addresses the problem by systematically reducing the maximum overlap through iterative improvements and adaptive grid resolution.  \n\n2. **Mathematical Specification**  \n   - **Variables**: Represent _h_ as a vector $\\mathbf{h} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ with $n$ grid points. The integral constraint is $\\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{h}[i] = \\frac{n}{2}$ (ensuring $\\int_0^2 \\text{ } \\mathrm{d}x \\boldsymbol{h}\\left(x\\right) = 1$).  \n   - **Objective**: Minimize $\\max_k \\int_0^2 \\mathrm{d}x \\, \\boldsymbol{h}\\left(x\\right) \\left(1 - \\boldsymbol{h}\\left(x + k\\right)\\right)$, where the index $k$ is discretized by $\\texttt{np.correlate}$ with full mode.  \n   - **Constraints**: $0 \\leq \\mathbf{h}[i] \\leq 1$ for all $i$, and $\\sum \\mathbf{h}[i] = \\frac{n}{2}$.  \n   - **Optimization Variant**: Piecewise constant $\\boldsymbol{h}$ functions are projected to new grid sizes by halving each component's value (ensuring boundary conditions are preserved).  \n\n3. **Implementation Procedure**  \n   - **Unit Handling**: Grid sizes double from $45$ (initial parent). Each iteration validates internal grid projection using equation (1).  \n   - **Search Algorithm**: Simulated annealing:  \n     - Target temperature: initialized to $5.0$, decreasing logarithmically.  \n     - Move deferral probability $\\Delta \\accept{}^{(t)} = \\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\text{score}_{w}\\left(\\boldsymbol{h}_{\\text{current}}\\right) - \\text{score}_{w}\\left(\\boldsymbol{h}_{\\text{candidate}}\\right)}{\\tau}\\right)$ if candidate score is worse.  \n     - **Repair**: For $\\text{swap}$ moves, ensure component values remain in $[0,1]$.  \n     - **Update Rhythm**: Steps escalated with temperature reduction. Grid doubling triggers when annealing schedule misses target improvements.  \n\n4. **Search Choices and Variations**  \n   - **Moves**: $\\text{swap}$ operators dominate (swaps of two grid indices).  \n   - **Annealing**: Temperature profile fixed by time. $\\Delta$ threshold $= 0.0001$.  \n   - **Dimensions**: Project for $n_{\\text{new}} = 2 \\times n_{\\text{old}}}$.  \n   - **Milestone**: Achieve $n_{\\text{total}} = 90$ for competitive timing.  \n\n5. **Computational Budget and Robustness**  \n   - **Cost**: $\\tilde{O}(\\max(n_t) \\log \\max(n_t))$ per evaluate (FFT-based).  \n   - **High-Level Logic**:  \n     - Time-out detection via $\\texttt{time.time()}$.  \n     - Exit gracefully if sample space not explored.  \n     - Handle solver errors by falling back to original parent.  \n\n6. **Validation and Return Contract**  \n   - **Feasibility**: Post-projection guarantee $\\boldsymbol{h} \\in [0,1]^{n}$, $\\sum \\boldsymbol{h} = n/2$.  \n   - **Output**: Integral $C_5$ computed via $\\texttt{np.correlate}$ scaled by $2/n$.",
